## Good vs Bad URI Design

```
// BAD — verbs in URIs:
GET  /api/getOrder?orderId=42
POST /api/createOrder
POST /api/deleteOrder?id=42
POST /api/updateOrderStatus

// GOOD — nouns + HTTP methods convey action:
GET    /api/orders/42       → Get order 42
POST   /api/orders          → Create new order
DELETE /api/orders/42       → Delete order 42
PATCH  /api/orders/42       → Update order 42 (partial)
PUT    /api/orders/42       → Replace order 42 (full update)
```

## Resource Hierarchy

```
// Collections and instances:
GET  /api/users                    → List all users
POST /api/users                    → Create user
GET  /api/users/{id}               → Get user by ID
PUT  /api/users/{id}               → Replace user
DELETE /api/users/{id}             → Delete user

// Nested resources (owned by parent):
GET  /api/users/{userId}/orders             → Orders for user
POST /api/users/{userId}/orders             → Create order for user
GET  /api/users/{userId}/orders/{orderId}   → Specific order for user

// Avoid deep nesting — prefer flat with filter:
// BAD:  GET /api/users/5/orders/42/items/7
// GOOD: GET /api/order-items/7  (if items exist independently)
//   OR: GET /api/orders/42/items/7 (if items only exist in order context)

// Sub-resources for actions:
POST /api/orders/42/cancel         → Cancel order (action as sub-resource)
POST /api/orders/42/ship           → Ship order
POST /api/users/5/deactivate       → Deactivate user
```

## Spring MVC Mapping

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/orders")
public class OrderController {

    @GetMapping
    public Page<OrderResponse> listOrders(
            @RequestParam(required = false) OrderStatus status,
            @RequestParam(required = false) Long userId,
            Pageable pageable) {
        return orderService.search(status, userId, pageable);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponse getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.findById(id));
    }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> createOrder(@Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
        Order order = orderService.create(req);
        URI location = ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentRequest()
            .path("/{id}")
            .buildAndExpand(order.getId())
            .toUri();
        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(orderMapper.toResponse(order));
        // Returns 201 Created with Location: /api/v1/orders/123
    }

    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponse replaceOrder(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @Valid @RequestBody UpdateOrderRequest req) {
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.replace(id, req));
    }

    @PatchMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponse updateOrder(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @RequestBody Map<String, Object> updates) {
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.partialUpdate(id, updates));
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
    public void deleteOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        orderService.delete(id);
    }

    // Action sub-resource:
    @PostMapping("/{id}/cancel")
    public OrderResponse cancelOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.cancel(id));
    }
}
```

## Query Parameters vs Path Parameters

```
// Path parameter: identifies a specific resource
GET /orders/42          → Resource identity — order 42

// Query parameter: filters, sorts, paginates a collection
GET /orders?status=PENDING&userId=5&sortBy=createdAt&page=0&size=20

// Rules:
// - Required identifier? → path parameter
// - Optional filter/sort? → query parameter
// - Pagination? → query parameter (page, size, cursor)

// Examples:
GET /products?category=electronics&minPrice=100&maxPrice=500&sort=price,asc
GET /search?q=spring+boot&type=books
GET /reports/daily?date=2024-01-15  // date is required context, not filter → could be path
GET /reports?type=daily&date=2024-01-15  // explicit filter
```

## Production Reality

Consistency is more important than perfection. Pick a naming convention (plural nouns, hyphens, lowercase) and stick to it across all endpoints. Document exceptions to the pattern. The worst APIs are inconsistent — some use camelCase, some snake_case, some verbs, some nouns. A style guide enforced via API review prevents this technical debt.

## DTO with Validation Constraints

```java
public class CreateOrderRequest {

    @NotNull(message = "User ID is required")
    private Long userId;

    @NotEmpty(message = "Order must have at least one item")
    @Size(max = 50, message = "Maximum 50 items per order")
    private List<@Valid OrderItemRequest> items;

    @NotNull(message = "Shipping address is required")
    @Valid  // Cascade validation to nested object
    private ShippingAddressRequest shippingAddress;

    @FutureOrPresent(message = "Requested delivery date must be in the future")
    private LocalDate requestedDelivery;

    @NotBlank
    @Pattern(regexp = "STANDARD|EXPRESS|OVERNIGHT",
             message = "Shipping method must be STANDARD, EXPRESS, or OVERNIGHT")
    private String shippingMethod;

    @DecimalMin(value = "0.01", message = "Promo code discount must be positive")
    @DecimalMax(value = "100.0", message = "Discount cannot exceed 100%")
    private BigDecimal promoDiscount;
    // null is valid (no promo) — use @NotNull if always required
}

public class OrderItemRequest {
    @NotNull
    private Long productId;

    @Min(value = 1, message = "Quantity must be at least 1")
    @Max(value = 999, message = "Quantity cannot exceed 999")
    private int quantity;
}
```

## Controller with @Valid

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
@Validated  // Required for method parameter validation (path vars, query params)
public class OrderController {

    // Validate request body:
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> createOrder(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
        // Runs ONLY if all validations pass
        // If any fail: MethodArgumentNotValidException → 400 response
        return ResponseEntity.ok(orderService.create(req));
    }

    // Validate path variable:
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponse getOrder(
            @PathVariable @Positive(message = "ID must be positive") Long id) {
        // @Validated on class enables this — throws ConstraintViolationException
        return orderMapper.toResponse(orderService.findById(id));
    }

    // Validate query parameters:
    @GetMapping
    public Page<OrderResponse> listOrders(
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") @Min(0) int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") @Min(1) @Max(100) int size) {
        return orderService.findAll(PageRequest.of(page, size));
    }
}
```

## Custom Cross-Field Validator

```java
// Validate that startDate is before endDate:
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Constraint(validatedBy = DateRangeValidator.class)
public @interface ValidDateRange {
    String message() default "Start date must be before end date";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};
}

public class DateRangeValidator
        implements ConstraintValidator<ValidDateRange, DateRangeRequest> {
    @Override
    public boolean isValid(DateRangeRequest req, ConstraintValidatorContext ctx) {
        if (req.getStartDate() == null || req.getEndDate() == null) return true;
        return req.getStartDate().isBefore(req.getEndDate());
    }
}

@ValidDateRange
public class DateRangeRequest {
    private LocalDate startDate;
    private LocalDate endDate;
}
```

## Validation Groups — For Different Scenarios

```java
// Different validations for create vs update:
public interface OnCreate {}
public interface OnUpdate {}

public class UserRequest {
    @Null(groups = OnCreate.class, message = "ID must not be provided on creation")
    @NotNull(groups = OnUpdate.class, message = "ID required for update")
    private Long id;

    @NotBlank(groups = {OnCreate.class, OnUpdate.class})
    private String name;

    @NotBlank(groups = OnCreate.class)  // Required on create, optional on update
    private String password;
}

@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity<User> createUser(
        @Validated(OnCreate.class) @RequestBody UserRequest req) { ... }

@PutMapping("/{id}")
public User updateUser(
        @PathVariable Long id,
        @Validated(OnUpdate.class) @RequestBody UserRequest req) { ... }
```

## Production Reality

Return all validation errors at once — not just the first failure. Your global exception handler for `MethodArgumentNotValidException` should collect all `FieldError` objects and return them as a map in the response. This lets the frontend display all invalid fields simultaneously, improving user experience. Never validate the same thing twice (once in controller, once in service) — each layer has its responsibility: controller validates format/structure, service validates business rules.

## Controller with Pageable

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {

    @GetMapping
    public Page<OrderResponse> listOrders(
            @RequestParam(required = false) OrderStatus status,
            @PageableDefault(size = 20, sort = "createdAt", direction = Sort.Direction.DESC)
            Pageable pageable) {
        // Automatically parsed from:
        // GET /api/orders?page=0&size=20&sort=createdAt,desc
        // GET /api/orders?page=2&size=10&sort=total,desc&sort=id,asc

        // Enforce max size (don't trust client):
        if (pageable.getPageSize() > 100) {
            pageable = PageRequest.of(pageable.getPageNumber(), 100, pageable.getSort());
        }

        Page<Order> orders = status != null
            ? orderRepo.findByStatus(status, pageable)
            : orderRepo.findAll(pageable);

        return orders.map(orderMapper::toResponse);
        // Page<Order> → Page<OrderResponse> (map content, keep metadata)
    }
}
```

## PageResponse DTO (Better Control)

```java
// Custom response wrapper:
public class PageResponse<T> {
    private List<T> content;
    private int page;          // Current page number (0-based)
    private int size;          // Page size
    private long totalElements;
    private int totalPages;
    private boolean first;
    private boolean last;
    private boolean empty;

    public static <T> PageResponse<T> from(Page<T> page) {
        PageResponse<T> response = new PageResponse<>();
        response.content = page.getContent();
        response.page = page.getNumber();
        response.size = page.getSize();
        response.totalElements = page.getTotalElements();
        response.totalPages = page.getTotalPages();
        response.first = page.isFirst();
        response.last = page.isLast();
        response.empty = page.isEmpty();
        return response;
    }
}

// Response example:
// {
//   "content": [...],
//   "page": 0,
//   "size": 20,
//   "totalElements": 1234,
//   "totalPages": 62,
//   "first": true,
//   "last": false
// }
```

## Repository Configuration

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // Spring Data auto-implements pagination:
    Page<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable);

    Page<Order> findByUserId(Long userId, Pageable pageable);

    @Query("SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.status = :status AND o.total > :minTotal")
    Page<Order> findByStatusAndMinTotal(
        @Param("status") OrderStatus status,
        @Param("minTotal") BigDecimal minTotal,
        Pageable pageable
    );
}
```

## Sorting Specification

```java
// Client-provided sort with validation:
@GetMapping
public Page<OrderResponse> listOrders(
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "createdAt") String sortBy,
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "desc") String direction) {

    // Whitelist allowed sort fields (prevent injection or exposure of internal fields)
    Set<String> allowedSortFields = Set.of("createdAt", "total", "status", "updatedAt");
    if (!allowedSortFields.contains(sortBy)) {
        throw new InvalidRequestException("Invalid sort field: " + sortBy);
    }

    Sort.Direction dir = "asc".equalsIgnoreCase(direction)
        ? Sort.Direction.ASC : Sort.Direction.DESC;
    Pageable pageable = PageRequest.of(0, 20, Sort.by(dir, sortBy));
    return orderRepo.findAll(pageable).map(orderMapper::toResponse);
}
```

## Production Reality

Never expose all sort fields — attackers can sort by internal fields to probe your data model or cause slow queries. Whitelist allowed sort fields. Always specify a tie-breaker sort (e.g., `sort=createdAt,desc&sort=id,desc`) to ensure deterministic ordering across pages — without it, database page boundaries are unstable.
